Although there are many ways this simultaneity can happen, economic theorists have made great strides in analyzing the simultaneity problem as it arises from equilibrium market relationships. In fact, 2SLS arose to understand agricultural markets. With a linear structure to supply and demand, we can even use algebra to solve for the equilibrium price and quantity analytically as \[\begin{aligned}
P^{*} &= \frac{\alpha_{D}-\alpha_{S}}{\beta_{D}+\beta_{S}} + \frac{E_{D} - E_{S}}{\beta_{D}+\beta_{S}}, \\
Q^{*} &= \frac{\alpha_{S}\beta_{D}+ \alpha_{D}\beta_{S}}{\beta_{D}+\beta_{S}} + \frac{E_{S}\beta_{D}+ E_{D}\beta_{S}}{\beta_{D}+\beta_{S}}.
\end{aligned}\]↩︎
Denoting \(X=[X_{1}, X_{2}]\), you estimate the OLS coefficients \(\hat{\mathbf{B}}\). After algebraic work, you can find the bias \(\mathbb{E}[ \hat{\mathbf{B}} - \beta]\) suffers from a contamination effect. \[\begin{aligned}
\hat{\mathbf{B}} &= [\hat{X}'\hat{X}]^{-1}X'y \\
\mathbb{E}[X'\epsilon] &=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 \\ \rho
\end{bmatrix} \\
\mathbb{E}[ \hat{\mathbf{B}} - \beta] &= [\hat{X}'\hat{X}]^{-1} \begin{bmatrix}
0 \\ \rho
\end{bmatrix} \neq
\begin{bmatrix}
0 \\ \rho
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}\] In words: when \(X_2\)’s row of \(X'\epsilon\) has expectation \(\rho\), the bias spreads through the inverse \((X'X)^{-1}\) onto every coefficient. That includes the one you cared about, \(X_1\).↩︎
